<BaseLayout title="Quantum Mechanics" description="States, operators, dynamics, spin, and identical particles for electronic-structure theory." current="theory">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/theory/')}>Theory</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Quantum Mechanics</span>
  </nav>

  <h1>Quantum Mechanics</h1>
  <p class="lede">Electronic structure begins when electrons are described by quantum states rather than classical trajectories. The central objects are states, operators, amplitudes, and the rules connecting them to measurable probabilities.</p>
  <p>This page establishes the quantum language needed before the many-electron problem is introduced. It does not attempt to replace a full sequence in quantum mechanics.</p>

  <h2>A state is more than a wave drawn in space</h2>
  <p>A pure state is represented by a ray in a complex inner-product space. A wavefunction is one representation of that state, just as a coefficient vector is another. Multiplying a state by an overall phase does not change its physical content.</p>
  <p>The coordinate representation connects the abstract ket to a complex-valued function:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="psi of r and t equals the position bra r applied to the state psi of t">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>ψ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <mo>⟨</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>⟩</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;,t)=\langle\mathbf&#123;r&#125;|\psi(t)\rangle.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Normalization fixes the total probability to one. Superposition means that linear combinations of allowed states are also allowed states; interference follows from adding amplitudes before taking their squared magnitude.</p>

  <h2>Dynamics is generated by the Hamiltonian</h2>
  <p>For a closed system, time evolution is governed by the time-dependent Schrödinger equation:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="i h bar partial derivative with respect to time of psi of t equals H of t psi of t">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>i</mi><mi>ℏ</mi>
          <mfrac><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac>
          <mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>⟩</mo>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <mover><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo>
          <mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>⟩</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\hbar\frac&#123;\partial&#125;&#123;\partial t&#125;|\psi(t)\rangle=\hat&#123;H&#125;(t)|\psi(t)\rangle.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>If the Hamiltonian is time independent, stationary states satisfy an eigenvalue equation. Their phase evolves in time while their probability density remains stationary. This distinction is essential when ground-state electronic structure is separated from explicitly time-dependent response.</p>

  <h2>Operators connect states to observables</h2>
  <p>An observable is represented by a self-adjoint operator. A measurement can return one of its spectral values, while repeated measurements on identically prepared systems are described statistically. The expectation value in a normalized state is</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="expectation value of A in psi equals psi bra A hat psi ket">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mo>⟨</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>⟩</mo><msub><mo></mo><mi>ψ</mi></msub>
          <mo>=</mo>
          <mo>⟨</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>|</mo><mover><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>|</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⟩</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle A\rangle_\psi=\langle\psi|\hat&#123;A&#125;|\psi\rangle.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Eigenvalues and expectation values answer different questions. An expectation value is generally not a value that every individual measurement must return.</p>

  <h2>Commutators express incompatible structure</h2>
  <p>The commutator <math class="math-inline" aria-label="commutator of A and B"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mover><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\hat&#123;A&#125;,\hat&#123;B&#125;]</annotation></semantics></math> tests whether two operators can be simultaneously simplified. Noncommuting observables obey uncertainty relations; the general Robertson bound is</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="uncertainty in A times uncertainty in B is greater than or equal to one half absolute value of the expectation of their commutator">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>Δ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>Δ</mi><mi>B</mi>
          <mo>≥</mo>
          <mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac>
          <mo>|</mo><mo>⟨</mo><mo>[</mo><mover><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mover><mi>B</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>]</mo><mo>⟩</mo><mo>|</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta A\,\Delta B\ge\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;2&#125;|\langle[\hat&#123;A&#125;,\hat&#123;B&#125;]\rangle|.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>This is a statement about state preparation and operator structure, not merely about imperfect instruments.</p>

  <h2>Angular momentum and spin are structural, not decorative</h2>
  <p>Orbital angular momentum organizes atomic and molecular states through rotational symmetry. Spin is an intrinsic two-component degree of freedom for an electron and cannot be replaced by picturing a small classical sphere rotating in space.</p>
  <p>Combining orbital and spin degrees of freedom requires tensor products. Coupling angular momenta, treating magnetic fields, and later introducing spin–orbit interaction all depend on this algebra.</p>

  <h2>Identical electrons require antisymmetry</h2>
  <p>Electrons are indistinguishable fermions. Exchanging every coordinate of two electrons changes the sign of the many-electron wavefunction:</p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Psi with electron coordinates i and j exchanged equals minus Psi with the original ordering">
      <semantics>
        <mrow>
          <mi>Ψ</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>)</mo>
          <mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo>
          <mi>Ψ</mi><mo>(</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo>
        </mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Psi(\ldots,x_i,\ldots,x_j,\ldots)=-\Psi(\ldots,x_j,\ldots,x_i,\ldots).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>This requirement produces exchange structure before any approximation is chosen. Slater determinants and the practical consequences for Hartree–Fock theory belong to the later many-electron pages.</p>

  <h2>The approximation tools that recur later</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Variational reasoning</strong> bounds the lowest energy within an admissible trial space.</li>
    <li><strong>Perturbation theory</strong> organizes response to a controlled change when the reference problem and expansion remain appropriate.</li>
    <li><strong>The adiabatic idea</strong> separates motions on different time scales, but its validity must be examined rather than assumed.</li>
    <li><strong>Density operators</strong> extend pure states to ensembles and partial information.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Open routes through the subject</h2>
  <p><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-04-quantum-physics-i-spring-2016/">MIT OpenCourseWare 8.04, Quantum Physics I</a> is the first open route through wavefunctions, operators, the Schrödinger equation, model potentials, tunnelling, and the harmonic oscillator.</p>
  <p><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-05-quantum-physics-ii-fall-2013/">MIT OpenCourseWare 8.05, Quantum Physics II</a> continues with bra–ket notation, spin, tensor products, angular momentum, and hydrogenic structure. It is the more direct bridge to atoms and many-electron theory.</p>
  <p><a href="https://academic.oup.com/book/43692">Jochen Autschbach, <cite>Quantum Theory for Chemical Applications</cite></a> provides a chemistry-oriented route from the same formalism to orbitals, bonding, perturbation theory, and spectroscopy. It is a commercial or institutionally accessed book, not an open replacement for the MIT sequence.</p>

  <h2>Boundary of this page</h2>
  <p>Knowing single-particle quantum mechanics does not solve interacting electrons. Antisymmetry, Coulomb interaction, reduced descriptions, mean-field approximations, density-functional foundations, periodic Bloch states, and quasiparticles each require further pages. The purpose here is to make their shared quantum language explicit.</p>

